---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import FileIntegrityComparison from '@/components/tools/security/FileIntegrityComparison.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="file-integrity-comparison">
  <FileIntegrityComparison client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to compare two files</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Drop one file into each panel. The order does not matter, and the files can have different names,
        folders and timestamps.
      </li>
      <li>
        Let the comparison run. The tool reads both files side by side in 4 MB slices, computes SHA-256 for
        each, and compares the bytes directly, all in one pass.
      </li>
      <li>
        Read the result. Identical means the contents are identical byte for byte. Different means at least
        one byte differs, and the panel tells you which one.
      </li>
      <li>
        Copy either digest if you need to paste it into a ticket, a release note or a message.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What is compared, and what is ignored</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Property</th>
          <th>Affects the verdict?</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>File contents (every byte)</td><td>Yes: this is the whole comparison.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>File length</td><td>Yes. Files of different lengths cannot be identical.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Filename</td><td>No.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Folder or drive</td><td>No.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Created and modified timestamps</td><td>No.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Permissions and ownership</td><td>No.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Extended attributes, resource forks, alternate data streams</td><td>No: the browser cannot see them.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Remember the last row on macOS and Windows: two files can have byte-identical contents and different
      metadata that the browser cannot access. For ordinary checks (did a copy survive, does a download
      match, did two backups diverge), the contents are what matter.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the first differing byte is useful</h2>
    <p>
      That two files differ tells you little. Where they first differ usually tells you why:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Offset 0: completely different files, or the same content in a different container format. Check
        the file signatures.
      </li>
      <li>
        A small offset, inside the header: embedded metadata such as a timestamp, a producer string, a
        compression level or a UUID from the exporting application. Common with PDFs, ZIPs and images.
      </li>
      <li>
        Deep into the file, with a matching prefix and one file shorter: a truncated or interrupted copy.
        The tool reports "beyond the shorter file" when one file is an exact prefix of the other, the
        classic sign of an incomplete download.
      </li>
      <li>
        Deep into the file, same length: one corrupted block, a bit flip from failing storage, or a
        targeted change. Isolated damage in the middle of a file that otherwise matches usually means a
        hardware problem, not an attack.
      </li>
      <li>
        At the very end: a trailing newline, an appended signature block, or padding from an archiver.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Common reasons two "identical" files differ</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Line endings: a text file that went through Windows has CRLF where the original had LF. Each line
        differs at the byte level, but the content reads the same.
      </li>
      <li>
        A trailing newline: editors disagree about whether the last line ends with one. One byte gives a
        completely different digest.
      </li>
      <li>
        Text encoding: UTF-8 with or without a byte-order mark. Three invisible bytes at offset 0.
      </li>
      <li>
        Timestamps inside archives: zipping the same directory twice gives different bytes, because the
        entries record the time of compression.
      </li>
      <li>
        Image and document metadata: EXIF, XMP and PDF producer strings change when a file is saved again,
        even with no visible edit.
      </li>
      <li>
        Silent corruption: rare, but real. Checksums exist to catch it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Handling very large files</h2>
    <p>
      Both files are streamed, not loaded, so peak memory stays at a few megabytes, for two text files or
      two 50 GB disk images. The limit is disk read speed, and the panel shows the combined throughput.
      Nothing is uploaded, so you can compare large private files; a server-based comparison would need
      both files transferred in full.
    </p>
    <p>
      SHA-256 is computed during the byte comparison, so you also get the digests. Two files with the same
      SHA-256 are identical for all practical purposes. The direct byte comparison lets the tool find the
      exact position of the difference.
    </p>

    <h2>Good uses for this</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Confirming that a backup or a restore is correct before you delete the original.</li>
      <li>Checking that a large file copied to an external drive or NAS arrived intact.</li>
      <li>Deciding if two similarly named files in your downloads folder are duplicates.</li>
      <li>Verifying that a build is reproducible: the same inputs gave the same artefact.</li>
      <li>Checking that an export from one tool matches an export from another.</li>
      <li>Confirming that a file you received matches the copy you already had.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
